From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Keith Packard <keithp@keithp.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:28:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] fpu: Normalize m68k unnormal operands

m68k accepts unnormals (a non-zero exponent with the explicit integer
bit clear) as valid operands, and floatx80_unpack_canonical lets them
through when floatx80_unnormal_valid is set. parts_canonicalize then
treated them like normal numbers and set the integer bit, so an
unnormal such as 3fff:4000000000000000 (0.5) was used as 1.5.

Normalize the fraction instead, adjusting the exponent, and treat an
unnormal with a zero fraction as zero, as the 68881 does.

Signed-off-by: Keith Packard <keithp@keithp.com>
---
 fpu/softfloat-parts.c.inc | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fpu/softfloat-parts.c.inc b/fpu/softfloat-parts.c.inc
index deb712e..8fef33e 100644
--- a/fpu/softfloat-parts.c.inc
+++ b/fpu/softfloat-parts.c.inc
@@ -260,6 +260,22 @@ static void partsN(canonicalize)(FloatPartsN *p, float_status *status,
     p->cls = float_class_normal;
     p->exp -= fmt->exp_bias;
     fracN(shl)(p, fmt->frac_shift);
+    if (unlikely(fmt->has_explicit_bit &&
+                 !(p->frac_hi & DECOMPOSED_IMPLICIT_BIT))) {
+        /*
+         * An unnormal: the explicit integer bit is clear although the
+         * exponent is not zero. Only targets which accept unnormals
+         * as valid input (m68k) get here; the value is the fraction
+         * scaled by the exponent, so normalize it rather than
+         * assuming an integer bit.
+         */
+        if (fracN(eqz)(p)) {
+            p->cls = float_class_zero;
+        } else {
+            p->exp -= fracN(normalize)(p);
+        }
+        return;
+    }
     p->frac_hi |= DECOMPOSED_IMPLICIT_BIT;
 }
 
